
# <type>(<scope>): <description> (#<request>)
#
# <summary: what the change does, in a sentence or two>
# <reason: what prompted it, where that is not obvious from the change>
# <outcome: what is different now — behaviour, contract, performance>
#
# BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and what callers must do instead>
# Decision: <a choice made on purpose> — <why>
# Refs: #<issue>
# Co-Authored-By: <name> <email>
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# subject     one line, precise: what changed, not how. Lowercase, no full stop, ~50 chars
# type        feat fix refactor perf revert docs test chore ci build
# scope       the module (request, ci, release, core, ...), or the area of work outside one
# (#request)  the request number. It is not known while the branch is being written — it exists
#             only once the request is open, and reaches main through the squash message
# Refs        the GitHub issue, when the work has one. The subject carries no issue number, so
#             it is never mistaken for the request number
# body        the details the subject cannot hold. Required for anything non-trivial
# breaking    also mark the subject: feat(request)!: ...
# Everything is English: subject, body and footers.
# Full rules: docs/git-flow.md
